        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Fixing confusion around id versus name]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/40264</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/content/pull/40264"/>
        <updated>2025-07-08T19:57:22.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/150330?u=41aa48771028d34bcf025eefe919bf75fbda77d4&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/><h3 dir="auto">Description</h3>
<p dir="auto">Clarifying the roles of <code class="notranslate">id</code> (aka "username") vs <code class="notranslate">name</code> (aka "display name") in this credential type, aligning them to typical expectations for username/password credentials.</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Motivation</h3>
<p dir="auto">The existing documentation (and sample code) implies confusing/mixed things about the roles of <code class="notranslate">id</code> versus <code class="notranslate">name</code> in the creation and storage of this credential type. Some parts of this page were accurate (to spec) but other places implied an opposite relationship that contradicts the spec (and browser behavior).</p>
<p dir="auto">I misunderstood after reading this page, and only figured it out after live testing -- including actually using <code class="notranslate">navigator.credentials.store()</code> to prompt to store the credential in the browser -- and reading the specification.</p>
<p dir="auto">So I want to fix the page so other's aren't misled like I was. :)</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Additional details</h3>
<p dir="auto">n/a</p>
<h3 dir="auto">Related issues and pull requests</h3>
<p dir="auto">n/a</p>]]></content>
